<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">St. Rumbold's Cathedral</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Saint Rumbold's Cathedral</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">St. Rumbold's Cathedral is the Gothic cathedral of Mechelen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, seat of the Archbishop of Mechelen-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>. Built mainly between the 13th and 15th centuries and dedicated to the early medieval missionary Saint Rumbold, it is known for its massive west tower, nearly 100 metres high, which was never crowned with its planned spire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/st-rumbolds-cathedral.jpg" alt="St. Rumbold's Cathedral" width="320" height="433" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User Jeantosti · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St-Romboutskathedraal3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Construction of the church started shortly after 1200, and it was consecrated in 1312, when part of it had become usable. From 1324 the flying buttresses and a revised choir took on Brabantine Gothic characteristics, distinct from French Gothic. After the city fire of 1342 the Master Mason Jean d'Oisy managed repairs and continued this second phase, and by his death in 1375 it had formed the prototype for that High Gothic style. His successors finished the vaults of the nave by 1437 and those of the choir by 1451.</p><p>The tower was erected in the final phase of 1452–1520, financed by pilgrims and later by the city. The church has been a cathedral since 1559, and in the 18th century the sculpted cabbage leaves round each capital were replaced with a double ring of crops.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The tower was designed to reach 600 Mechlinian feet, about 167 metres, and was built on what had been wetlands with foundations only three metres deep. Its upper part was abandoned in the early 16th century for financial reasons, not technical ones. It should have been topped by a 77-metre spire, but only seven metres were built, which gives it its flat-topped shape.</p><p>The tower is 97.28 metres high with 514 steps. For centuries it held the city documents, served as a watchtower and sounded the fire alarm. It contains 49 bells, of which the six largest swing. The bourdon Salvator weighs 8 tons and strikes the hours. The nave is about 118 metres long, and the interior has a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> high altar and choir by Lucas Faydherbe, paintings by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anthony-van-dyck">Anthony van Dyck</a> and stained-glass windows.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The tower was inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List in 1999, as part of the Belfries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> site. Few original furnishings survive: forty decorated Gothic altars and all other furniture disappeared during the religious troubles of 1566–1585. The cathedral was spared in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iconoclasm">Iconoclasm</a> of 1566, but Mechelen was sacked in the Spanish Fury of 1572 and suffered the English Fury of pillaging by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> in the service of the States General in 1580.</p><p>In 2010, 4,165 skeletons were unearthed in excavations of the cemetery before an underground car park was built. In 1985 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-paul-ii">Pope John Paul II</a> said Mass there, and Jo Haazen, the city's carillon player, heard him say, 'Your tower is not complete.' Saint Rumbold's relics were examined and indicated a death date between 580 and 655, while tradition had claimed 775.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “St. Rumbold's Cathedral” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mechelen was a prosperous cloth town with a tall cathedral tower of <b>St Rumbold</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-mechelen">Lordship of Mechelen</a></li></ul></section>
